template Docs {

  // The kinds reference page of the website (spec B.2). `language markdown` since firn v0.2.0 (si#410);
  // v0.1.0 had none (E200) and this said `html`, whose id line is the same html comment - the page came
  // out byte-identical under both. A line starting with `|` in the output needs `||`, because the first
  // `|` is the template's output marker.
  file "kinds.md" language markdown {
    |---
    |title: "Artifact kinds"
    |weight: 6
    |---
    |
    |GENERATED from `src/firn/model/simplon/vocabulary/kinds.firn` (the kinds) and
    |`src/firn/model/simplon/catalogue/tasks.firn` (the ports) by `./simplon.sh build model`.
    |
    |An artifact names its kind; a port accepts a kind and every kind that refines it.
    |
    || Kind | Refines |
    ||---|---|
    for k : `simplon.pipeline.ArtifactKind` {
      if k.refines {
        || `${k.name.replace("_", "-")}` | <<for r : k.refines sep ", ">>`${r.name.replace("_", "-")}`<<end>> |
      }
      if not k.refines {
        || `${k.name.replace("_", "-")}` |  |
      }
    }
    |
    |## Ports
    |
    |A catalogue task takes and gives artifacts through its ports. A port accepts a kind and every kind that
    |refines it; a port that binds a parameter receives the bound artifact's name in it.
    |
    |A command binds a port with `produces: { <port>: <artifact> }` on an output and
    |`consumes: { <port>: <artifact> }` on an input. The artifact is an `artifacts:` entry whose kind the port accepts.
    |A port that binds a parameter hands the artifact's name to it; `with: { name: ... }` on that parameter is read as
    |the same binding, and `simplon manifest migrate` rewrites it into the port's.
    |
    || Task | Port | Direction | Accepts | Binds |
    ||---|---|---|---|---|
    for t : `simplon.pipeline.CatalogueTask` {
      for p : t.input {
        if p.param {
          || `${t.key}` | `${p.name.replace("_", "-")}` | input | `${p.accepts.name.replace("_", "-")}` | `${p.param}` |
        }
        if not p.param {
          || `${t.key}` | `${p.name.replace("_", "-")}` | input | `${p.accepts.name.replace("_", "-")}` |  |
        }
      }
      for p : t.output {
        if p.param {
          || `${t.key}` | `${p.name.replace("_", "-")}` | output | `${p.accepts.name.replace("_", "-")}` | `${p.param}` |
        }
        if not p.param {
          || `${t.key}` | `${p.name.replace("_", "-")}` | output | `${p.accepts.name.replace("_", "-")}` |  |
        }
      }
    }
    |
    |**A release consumes the results of the tests on what it publishes.** Every publish leaf has an `evidence` input
    |port accepting `test-result`. A command under `ci: release:` that consumes an artifact must bind
    |every test result about it there, and at least one must exist (rule 6). A test result is about what its producer consumes, directly:
    |a unit test of the source is not a test of the image built from it. The notes, the tag, the bundle and the evidence
    |itself need none. One widening (si#789): an artifact bound to an input port that verifies against the evidence
    |(`release:image`'s `archive`, the only one) is covered by the tests about an artifact the same command produced
    |beside it, when the release consumes that one too - the body holds the archive's images against what the test ran
    |before it pushes. On every other port rule 6 is as strict as without it.
    |
    |The evidence is read before anything is published (si#434). The load checks the binding; at run time every bound
    |record is read before the publish body runs, and a record that is missing, red or without a verdict stops it.
    |`release:image` also compares what the record tested with what it would push (si#439): each run names the
    |reference and the daemon id it tested, so an image never tested under that tag, or rebuilt since, is refused.
  }

  // The auto-merge policy page (si#874): the merge policy's rules, token scopes and script inputs, read off its one
  // instance, so the `merge:` section of how/manifest.md links here instead of carrying a typed copy.
  file "auto-merge.md" language markdown {
    for p : `simplon.automerge.MergePolicy` {
      |---
      |title: "The auto-merge policy"
      |weight: 8
      |---
      |
      |GENERATED from `src/firn/model/simplon/automerge/automerge.firn` by `./simplon.sh build model`.
      |
      |What `merge: auto` switches on (how/manifest.md, section `merge:`): the derived
      |`.github/workflows/auto-merge.yml` merges an open pull request labelled `${p.label}` with `gh pr merge --${p.method}`
      |once these rules pass. The generated workflow carries the same reason beside each rule, and its script is
      |generated from the same instance.
      |
      |## The rules
      |
      |They run in this order. A rule that holds the merge says why in the log; a failed read is red, never a green
      |"nothing to do".
      |
      || Rule | Why | Source |
      ||---|---|---|
      for r : p.rules {
        || `${r.key}` | ${r.reason} | ${r.source} |
      }
      |
      |## The token
      |
      |The workflow's `permissions:` block is a strict allowlist, so each scope is written with its reason, in the
      |generated file as here.
      |
      || Scope | Why |
      ||---|---|
      for q : p.permissions {
        || `${q.scope}: ${q.access}` | ${q.reason} |
      }
      |
      |## The script's inputs
      |
      |The merge step's `env:`, in the order the step writes it, and the script's whole interface: it reads nothing
      |else, so nothing a pull request author controls is written into it. Each value is a GitHub expression, or a
      |field of the derived workflow model the kernel fills in. A required input that is empty is red before any call.
      |
      for i : p.inputs {
        if i.expression {
          if i.required {
            |- `${i.variable}` (required): ${i.meaning}. From `${i.expression}`.
          }
          if not i.required {
            |- `${i.variable}`: ${i.meaning}. From `${i.expression}`.
          }
        }
        if not i.expression {
          if i.required {
            |- `${i.variable}` (required): ${i.meaning}. From the derivation (`${i.source}`).
          }
          if not i.required {
            |- `${i.variable}`: ${i.meaning}. From the derivation (`${i.source}`).
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
